A||||11||westtexas||.95 G cornering power, easy or hard to get? ||||1||
Z||000000||westtexas||08-03-2001||11:06 AM||||I bought my Fiero, an '86 GT, sight unseen off Ebay because I thought it looked great, really didn't know anything about Fieros. Eight months later and I have four and am working on a 4.9 Cadillac swap. I would like know the potential of the Fiero and how to achieve it. I know that the '88 cradle is superior and autocross alignment can make a big difference. So, if I go to the '88 cradle, urethane bushings, 16" wheels with high performance tires, good shocks, and ??? will I end up with handling that only a few cars on the street can beat? With that kind of suspension will I get beat up inside?<P>Thanks for your help,<BR>Robert Jennings||||reg||1
Z||000001||lowCG||08-03-2001||11:36 AM||||Welcome to the forum.<BR>If you'd gotten an '88 model,I'd say that's great to have with it's superior goemetry,but I wouldn't bother swapping or anything.<BR>You can get great handling with a set of Eibach springs,Koni shocks,alignment and a set of good tires;better than you can really use on the street to full potential anyways.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-03-2001||11:37 AM||||my 86 SE would hold 1.13g on a 200' skid pad at about 64ish-mph. it rode fine.. so fine i drove it from atl to south dakota, and never once had a headache.. since they are adjustable the squishy ride around town is sweet.. then 20 seconds later after the turn of a knob your on rails again... my GT is similar.. but rides much worse due to the lower aspect ratio of the tires<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||KissMySSFiero||08-03-2001||12:13 PM||||1fst,<BR>are you using konis on your car?  <BR>and what else have you done to your suspension?  Poly, springs, coilovers, etc?<BR>TIA<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000004||westtexas||08-03-2001||12:42 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by KissMySSFiero:<BR>[B]1fst,<BR>are you using konis on your car?  <BR>and what else have you done to your suspension?  Poly, springs, coilovers, etc?<BR>TIA<P>Basically, I'm a very green mechanic. It took me several weeks to get the cradle out of my '87 (4.9 swap learning tool/victim). The car is sorta scattered around the shop. It was going to be completely stock<BR> except for the 4.9. But I have read a few road tests of new import sedans doing 14.9 in the quarter and .9 on the skid pad. I'd like to have a faster/better handling car then most of the cars I'll meet on the street. I did rile a new Vette the other day and he blew me off quick, in fact the last I saw the Harley after beating him across the intersection was a little speck disaapearing in the long, straight highway going to Odessa. I don't have to have the quickest but I want to have one of the best handling. If list members get over one G, I know I have the right car. 1.13 is like having little hooks in the road. <P>Robert<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||08-03-2001||02:17 PM||||The Fiero has insane cornering potential, new koni coil overs  all around, (and optionally drop spindles in front help a lot.) Poly bushings, and a good set of 16" or 17" wheels, and the most important part, *grippy* tyres, and you will pull over 1G all day long.  Sometimes quite a bit more than 1G...<P>The Fiero's incredibly low stock cg and ride height and mid-engine layout are great starting points.<P>The 88s are best, though you can get great handling out of a previous year car, but at the sacrafice of comfort (you basically have to have a rock hard suspension to avoid the nasties in the earlier cars' travel.  The 88s can have a more compliant setup that is also even more agile.)<P>Note that if you do not have an 88 car, I would recomend a brake upgrade.  I would skip all of the half-a** "grand am" etc swaps, and go straight to a Ryane/Held Motorsports 12 or 13" setup.  Especially with your V8, it is essential for safe driving.<P>The Caddy 4.9 engine will be great fun :)<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000006||maryjane||08-05-2001||02:01 AM||||Welcome to the forum. Just wondering where you have many turns to make in Andrews? Plannin a high speed run thru downtown Midland? And you've got how many Fieros? Now I know where all the ones from Angelo have disappeared to.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">btw, I know where there is one in an abandoned junkyard. Been trying to track down the owner but heard he's working on a ranch out around McCamey<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||08-05-2001||08:42 AM||||Hey Texas!<BR>I didn't see any mention of sway bars...<BR>It was THE single best suspention mod I did to my Fiero, and that was to add a rear sway bar...<BR>On my first Fiero, I did the Poly, shocks, and springs, but it didn't add up to the difference of just the sway bar and stock suspention on my second Fiero...<BR>Beware of alignment shops, I have had them loosen bolts and claim my Fiero was trashed out suspention wise...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tina||08-05-2001||09:21 AM||||O.K. guys, you got me.<P>How do you measure lateral G's?<P>Tina||||reg||1
Z||000009||westtexas||08-05-2001||10:28 AM||||Ben, thanks for the advice on the brakes. I bought a new-in-the-box Speedway kit for $500 in Odessa yesterday. The Ryan kit brake kit is almost as much as I spent on my GT (but it is beautiful). <P>Don, I bought my '86 GT with 54,500 miles in Abilene and '84 2M4 in San Angelo. I just bought a '88 2.5/auto in Mesa, Az. yesterday (may put my 4.9 in it instead of the '87). That makes five Fieros. Bought first one last Nov. (I've got a problem.) In Andrews there is a loop road with a sweeping 'S' bend. I can go 80 mph but now worried about oversteer. So, I want my 4.9 to handle fast turns predictablely. This is going to be fun. I really like road racing more than drag racing. <P>Thanks everybody for all the advice and information. I'm going to use it.<P>Robert Jennings<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||westtexas||08-05-2001||10:37 AM||||How do you measure lateral G's?<P>I assume with a GTech Pro meter. B F Goodrich is giving one away if you buy a set of 'H', 'V', or 'Z' rated tires by Aug. 31. That was one of the reasons I got interested in handling. Goodrich didn't have any high performance tires in 14 inch and I wanted that GTech. Now looking for 1992 Beretta 16" alloy wheels. None in Odessa junkyards, Lubbock next.<P>I sure am glad the forum is back up, Thanks Cliff.<P>Robert Jennings <P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Will||08-05-2001||11:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B>Hey Texas!<BR>I didn't see any mention of sway bars...<BR>It was THE single best suspention mod I did to my Fiero, and that was to add a rear sway bar...<BR>On my first Fiero, I did the Poly, shocks, and springs, but it didn't add up to the difference of just the sway bar and stock suspention on my second Fiero...<BR>Beware of alignment shops, I have had them loosen bolts and claim my Fiero was trashed out suspention wise...<BR>crash...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>For controlability's sake I would suggest poly/aluminum cradle bushings and poly/delrin rear control arm bushings first, then add the rear bar.<BR>With that setup, you can actually make excellent use of the Fiero's inherent lift-off reaction.||||reg||1
